{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "קום לך למסע", :translit "qum lekh la-masa", :gloss "Arise, go on the journey"} {:he "ג' תגי\"ן על קו\"ף", :translit "g' tagin al qof", :gloss "three tagin on the qof"} {:he "שאזכור להם זכות אברהם", :translit "she-ezkor lahem zekhut avraham", :gloss "that I may remember for them the merit of Abraham"} {:he "קום התהלך בארץ לארכה ולרחבה כי לך אתננה", :translit "qum hit'halekh ba-aretz le-or'khah u-le-r'khavah ki le-kha etnenah", :gloss "Arise, walk about the land, its length and its breadth, for to you I will give it"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["תגין on the קו\"ף of קום in Deuteronomy 10:11"]} {:kind :other, :note "Three tagin on the qof of קום recall the three expressions in God's charge to Abraham in Genesis 13:17"}], :frame {:text "The verse 'Arise, walk about the land' (Genesis 13:17) records God's command to Abraham to traverse the land of Canaan, which constitutes a foundational act of inheritance. The parallel phrasing 'Arise' (קום) in both Genesis 13:17 and Deuteronomy 10:11 links Israel's journey to Abraham's original walk.", :sources ["Genesis 13:17" "Deuteronomy 10:11"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אשר נשבעתי לאבותם לתת להם", :translit "asher nishba'ti la-avotam latet lahem", :gloss "which I swore to their fathers to give them"} {:he "ועתה ישראל מה ה' אלהיך שואל מעמך כי אם ליראה", :translit "ve-atah yisra'el mah Hashem elohekha sho'el mei-imekha ki im le-yir'ah", :gloss "And now, Israel, what does Hashem your God ask of you, but to fear"} {:he "לומר לך שהאבות היו יראי ה'", :translit "le'omar l'kha she-ha-avot hayu yir'ei Hashem", :gloss "to say to you that the fathers were fearers of Hashem"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "S'mukhin: adjacent verses linked — Deuteronomy 10:11 ends with the fathers and 10:12 opens demanding fear, implying the fathers possessed that fear"}], :frame {:text "The principle of s'mukhin (adjacency) links neighboring verses to derive meaning: since Deuteronomy 10:12 states 'What does Hashem ask of you, but to fear' immediately after Deuteronomy 10:11 references 'the fathers,' the juxtaposition implies the patriarchs exemplified fear of God. That the patriarchs were characterized by fear of God is established by verse: Genesis 22:12 declares Abraham a fearer of God.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 10:11" "Deuteronomy 10:12" "Genesis 22:12"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["תגין on the קו\"ף of קום in Deuteronomy 10:11"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "תגין on the קו\"ף of קום in Deuteronomy 10:11", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "Three tagin on the qof of קום recall the three expressions in God's charge to Abraham in Genesis 13:17", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "S'mukhin: adjacent verses linked — Deuteronomy 10:11 ends with the fathers and 10:12 opens demanding fear, implying the fathers possessed that fear", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:35:47.955141171Z", :model "glm-5.2"}